=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=
=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=C2=A0=E2=80=8C=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0A=
By Bill Murphy =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Powered by Colony Spark [https://www.colo=
nyspark.com/]=0A=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS=0ABRIEF=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AISSUE #19 =C2=
=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 SEPTEMBER 18, 2026=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AHi ${first_=
name},=0A=0AA plant manager types a question into the new ERP chat box and =
gets an answer in=0Afour seconds. The number is clean, the sentence around =
it is confident, and=0Anobody in the room can tell you which of a dozen unv=
oted decisions produced it.=0ANatural language changed how fast you can ask=
. It did not decide what the answer=0Ameans.=0A=0AThis issue is about what=
 has to be true before you trust a conversational answer=0Aout of the ERP: =
a roundtable operator's rule for proving a number before=0Aexplaining it, a=
nd a one-page metric constitution you can fill in this week for=0Athe one K=
PI everyone in the room already argues about.=0A=0ABill=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATH=
E SHIFT=0A=0A=0ANatural-Language ERP Needs a Metric Constitution.=0A=0AConv=
ersational access changes the cost of asking. It does not settle what the=
=0Aquestion means.=0A=0APicture the question every operations leader eventu=
ally types into a chat box:=0Awhat shipped late last month=3F The system th=
at used to require a saved search and=0Aa report writer now returns an answ=
er in seconds. What it does not surface is=0Athe list of choices baked into=
 that answer: promised date or requested date,=0Aorder level or line level,=
 whether a partial shipment counts as late, whether a=0Areopened order rese=
ts the clock, whether a cancelled line ever enters the=0Adenominator, which=
 plant's production calendar applies, and how stale the=0Aunderlying data i=
s at the moment you asked.=0A=0AThat is the trade natural-language ERP is m=
aking across the industry right now.=0AOracle's Ask Oracle lets eligible Ne=
tSuite Next users ask business questions in=0Aplain English and pull an ans=
wer straight out of the system. Oracle's own=0Adocumentation is candid abou=
t the risk: vague terms such as open or delayed can=0Aproduce unexpected re=
sults, and when the system is uncertain, it appends an=0Ainterpretation not=
e explaining what it assumed. That is a useful safety valve.=0AIt is also a=
n admission that the tool cannot read your mind about what open=0Aactually =
means at your plant.=0A=0AFour data points behind the shift=0A=0A=C2=B7 Ora=
cle's own guidance warns that ambiguous terms like open or delayed can lead=
=0Ato unexpected results, so Ask Oracle appends an interpretation note when=
 it is=0Auncertain (Oracle Ask Oracle documentation=0A[https://docs.oracle.=
com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/article_5100902387.html]).=0A=0A=
=C2=B7 NetSuite's August 2026 release lets admins manage Ask Oracle access =
by role,=0Ause permitted saved searches as answer sources, and preview NetS=
uite Next on a=0Acopy of live account data (Oracle NetSuite 2026.2 release =
notes=0A[https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/arti=
cle_1175030259.html]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 NIST's July 2026 smart-manufacturing roa=
dmap names industrial data management,=0Aintegration across heterogeneous s=
ystems, and trustworthy, explainable, reliable=0Aoperation as critical barr=
iers to AI deployment on the floor (NIST=0A[https://www.nist.gov/publicatio=
ns/2026-roadmap-artificial-intelligence-and-machine-learning-smart-manufact=
uring]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 Deloitte's 2026 survey of more than 140 manufacturers =
found 84% seeing=0Ameasurable value from AI, while only about 20% of use ca=
ses are actually scaled,=0Aciting robust data and governance as the gap (De=
loitte=0A[https://www.deloitte.com/de/de/Industries/industrial-construction=
/research/ai-in-manufacturing.html]).=0A=0ATurn those August controls on. T=
hen be honest about what they do. Role-based=0Aaccess decides who is allowe=
d to ask. Saved searches decide what context the=0Aanswer is allowed to dra=
w from. The sandbox preview decides who can break things=0Asafely. None of =
the three decides whether a partial shipment counts as late at=0Ayour plant=
. That is a business decision, it needs a named owner, and no release=0Ano=
te is going to make it for you.=0A=0ANIST's framing backs up the operationa=
l point: the barrier to trustworthy AI on=0Athe floor is not the model, it =
is the industrial data management and governance=0Alayer sitting underneath=
 it. Deloitte's numbers put a scale on the gap. Most=0Amanufacturers are al=
ready seeing value from AI. Most of that value is still=0Astuck in pilots, =
and the reason, in Deloitte's own words, comes down to robust=0Adata, gover=
nance, and deployment models. Read that as a scale-gap signal from a=0Abroa=
d, cross-industry study, not a market forecast for any one region.=0A=0ANon=
e of this is an argument against handing people a chat box instead of a=0Ar=
eport writer. It is an argument for certifying the handful of metrics that=
=0Aactually drive daily decisions before turning the chat box loose on all =
of them.=0AOn-time delivery, gross margin, backlog, late orders: pick the s=
hort list first,=0Aand settle it before the questions start arriving at con=
versational speed.=0A=0AThe fix is not fewer questions. It is fewer ungover=
ned answers.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFROM THE FLOOR=0A=0A=0AShow the proof before =
the reasoning.=0A=0ARobert Wyse, Industronics Service Company, on what it t=
akes to get an=0AAI-generated number trusted on the floor=0A=0ARobert Wyse =
owns Industronics Service Company. Speaking at the Colony Spark=0AOperators=
 Roundtable in August, he named the exact standard an AI-generated ERP=0Aan=
swer has to clear before anyone on the floor will act on it.=0A=0A=E2=80=9C=
I think you really have to show the proof before you get into the reasoning=
.=0AYou have to illustrate the why. Otherwise, it's really hard to get eve=
ryone on=0Aboard.=E2=80=9D=0A=0ARobert Wyse=0A=0AOwner, Industronics Servic=
e Company =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Colony Spark Operators Roundtable,=0AAugust 20=
26=0A=0AWyse is describing sequence, not sentiment. Show the proof first: t=
he=0Adefinition, the source records, the filters, the timestamp. Only then =
does the=0Areasoning get a hearing. Flip that order, lead with a confident-=
sounding=0Aexplanation before anyone has seen where the numbers came from, =
and the room=0Astops trusting the tool the first time the number does not m=
atch what the plant=0Amanager already knows.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0ABefore =
an AI-generated ERP answer changes a plan, a schedule, or a customer=0Acomm=
itment, the operator needs to see what counts, where the data came from, an=
d=0Aa way to check it against a report they already trust. Confidence is no=
t proof.=0AReproducibility is.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Metri=
c Constitution, Built in dbt.=0A=0ADefine the metric once. Expose only the =
certified answer.=0A=0AA one-page constitution per metric, a governed calcu=
lation layer between the=0Achat box and the warehouse, and a four-step rout=
ine for reconciling what it=0Areturns.=0A=0AThe problem in Section 1 has a =
fix that does not require blocking the chat box.=0ADefine the operational m=
etric once, in a governed semantic layer, then let the=0AAI agent query and=
 explain that certified definition instead of inventing its=0Aown. The dbt =
Semantic Layer paired with dbt MCP is the most specific version of=0Athis a=
vailable today for manufacturers and distributors whose ERP and WMS data=0A=
already land in a warehouse.=0A=0AThis setup centralizes the definitions of=
 the metrics that actually run the=0Aplant: on-time delivery, fill rate, in=
ventory turns, margin, supplier=0Aperformance. It separates the natural-lan=
guage interface from the calculation.=0AThe agent's job becomes explaining =
and querying an approved metric, not deciding=0Awhat late means on the fly.=
=0A=0AThe one-page metric constitution=0A=0ABefore any of this is code, it =
is a page. Pick the metric your team argues about=0Amost and answer these e=
ight lines. If you cannot answer one of them without a=0Ameeting, you have =
found the reason the chat box is going to embarrass someone.=0A=0A1. Name a=
nd plain-English definition. One sentence an operator would say out=0Aloud,=
 not a formula.=0A=0A2. Owner. A person, not a department. They settle disp=
utes about this number.=0A=0A3. Calculation and grain. The math, and whethe=
r it counts orders, lines, units,=0Aor dollars.=0A=0A4. Time dimension. Whi=
ch date field, and why that one instead of the other=0Athree.=0A=0A5. Filte=
rs and exclusions. Written down. Samples, intercompany, cancellations,=0Are=
work, test orders.=0A=0A6. Source of record. Which system and which table t=
he number comes from when two=0Adisagree.=0A=0A7. Refresh timing. How curre=
nt the data is at the moment someone asks, stated in=0Ahours.=0A=0A8. Recon=
ciles against. The one certified report this metric has to match, every=0At=
ime.=0A=0ALines 1 through 7 become the metric definition in dbt. Line 8 is =
what the=0Aoperator checks before acting. Here is the routine that moves th=
e page into the=0Astack.=0A=0ADefine, expose, ask, reconcile=0A=0A1. Define=
. Build the operational metric in dbt: calculation, time dimension,=0Aenti=
ties, grain, filters, exclusions. Test it against known results and deploy=
=0Ait to the Semantic Layer.=0A=0A2. Expose. Configure dbt MCP in the appro=
ved agent environment. Turn on the=0ASemantic Layer and only the metadata t=
ools the workflow needs, on the narrowest=0Apractical account and token per=
missions.=0A=0A3. Ask. Pose the operating question. Require the response to=
 show the metric=0Aname and definition, filters, grain, time range, and res=
ult, not just a number.=0A=0A4. Reconcile. Check the result against the cer=
tified report or a known query. If=0Athe numbers differ, the metric owner r=
esolves the definition, source, or filter=0Amismatch before anyone acts on =
it.=0A=0AExample=0A=0AAsk, why did on-time delivery fall at the East plant =
last month=3F The agent=0Aqueries the governed on-time-delivery metric, sta=
tes exactly how on time is=0Adefined, shows the plant and date filters and =
the result grain, then breaks the=0Aresult down only through approved dimen=
sions. The operator compares the total=0Aagainst the existing report before=
 using the explanation for anything.=0A=0AAvailability and guardrails=0A=0A=
Availability=0A=0AThe dbt Semantic Layer is available on dbt Platform Start=
er, Enterprise, and=0AEnterprise+ accounts. The self-hosted dbt MCP server =
can run locally, but its=0ASemantic Layer toolset needs dbt Platform creden=
tials and a production=0Aenvironment ID. Starter pricing runs $100 per user=
 per month with 5,000 queried=0Ametrics included (dbt Semantic Layer docs=
=0A[https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/use-dbt-semantic-layer/dbt-sl], dbt pricin=
g=0A[https://www.getdbt.com/pricing]).=0A=0AGuardrails=0A=0AKeep the agent =
scoped to the Semantic Layer and the metadata tools the workflow=0Aactually=
 needs. Do not enable SQL execution, administrative, job-management, or=0Aw=
rite-capable tools unless the use case and permissions explicitly call for =
them=0A(dbt MCP setup [https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/dbt-ai/setup-local-mcp]=
).=0A=0AThe interface can be conversational. The number it returns still ne=
eds a=0Aconstitution behind it.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS BRIEF=0A=0A=
By Bill Murphy =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Powered by Colony Spark [https://www.colo=
nyspark.com/]=0A=0AAlready treating your ERP metrics as a governed asset, o=
r watching a chat box=0Agenerate three different answers to the same questi=
on=3F Hit reply, I read every=0Aone. Bill
